Let $O$ be the origin, and let $M$ be the midpoint of $\overline{PQ}.$

[asy]
unitsize(3 cm);

pair A, M, O, P, Q, T;

real func (real x) {
  return ((x - 1)*(x - 2));
}

A = (1.5,-0.4);
O = (0,0);
M = (1.5,0);
P = (1,0);
Q = (2,0);
T = intersectionpoint(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))),arc(A/2,abs(A)/2,0,90));

draw(graph(func,0.5,2.5));
draw((-0.5,0)--(2.5,0));
draw((0,-1)--(0,1));
draw(Circle(A,abs(A - (1,0))));
draw(O--T);
draw(A--M);

label("$t$", T/3, N);
label("$M$", M, S);
label("$O$", O, SW);
label("$P$", P, S);
label("$Q$", Q, S);
label("$A$", A, SE);

dot(T);
[/asy]

Then by the Power of a Point Theorem, $t^2 = OM \cdot OA.$

Since the roots of $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ are $p$ and $q,$ $p + q = -\frac{b}{a}$ and $pq = \frac{c}{a}.$  Then
\[OM = \frac{p + q}{2} = -\frac{b}{2a}\]and $PM = \frac{q - p}{2}.$  Also,
\[AM = |a| \cdot PM^2 = |a| \cdot \frac{(q - p)^2}{4} = |a| \cdot \frac{(p + q)^2 - 4pq}{4} = \left| a \cdot \frac{\frac{b^2}{a^2} - \frac{4c}{a}}{4} \right| = \left| \frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a} \right|.\]Hence,
\[OA = AM - OM = \left| \frac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a} \right| + \frac{b}{2a} = \frac{|b^2 - 4ac| + 2b}{4|a|}.\]Therefore,
\[t^2 = OM \cdot OA = \boxed{\frac{-b(|b^2 - 4ac| + 2b)}{8a^2}}.\]TERMINATE

